<SiteLayout title="Module 10: Basic Router Configuration — fred.fish" description="Build a router configuration from initial hostname and security settings through interface addressing, default routing, and connectivity verification." active="cisco">
<section class="source-lesson">
<div class="lesson-nav"><a href="/cisco/learning/semester-1/">← Semester 1 course</a><a href="/cisco/practice/">Adaptive practice ↗</a></div>
<header class="lesson-hero"><Eyebrow index="S1 / 10" label="MODULE 10 / ROUTER IOS CONFIGURATION" /><h1>Configure the router.<br /><span>Verify the path.</span></h1><p>Build a router configuration from initial hostname and security settings through interface addressing, default routing, and connectivity verification.</p></header>
<section class="lesson-section"><div class="lesson-number">01</div><div><h2>Establish a safe baseline</h2><p>Set a meaningful hostname and IP domain name, protect privileged EXEC access with enable secret, create a local username with a secret, and secure console and VTY access. For SSH, generate RSA keys after setting the hostname/domain name, configure VTY lines with login local, and restrict transport input to SSH. Use a legal message-of-the-day banner. The legacy service password-encryption command applies reversible Type 7 obfuscation to certain plaintext line passwords; it is not strong modern encryption and does not replace enable secret or SSH. Test a login banner by opening a new session, not by rebooting the router.</p><div class="lesson-source"><span>SOURCE IMAGES</span><code>IMG-0692 · IMG-0698</code></div></div></section>
<section class="lesson-section"><div class="lesson-number">02</div><div><h2>Configure routed interfaces</h2><p>Enter interface configuration mode for each required router interface. Apply the IPv4 address and subnet mask from the topology/addressing table, add a useful description, and issue no shutdown when the interface should be enabled. Configure both LAN/WAN-facing interfaces as required; do not copy an address or mask from the wrong subnet. A router interface must be correctly addressed and operational before it can route traffic for that network.</p><div class="lesson-source"><span>SOURCE IMAGES</span><code>IMG-0713 · IMG-0720</code></div></div></section>
<section class="lesson-section"><div class="lesson-number">03</div><div><h2>Configure and verify routing</h2><p>Hosts use the router interface on their own subnet as the default gateway. A Layer 2 switch can use ip default-gateway for management traffic sent to other IP networks; this is not a routed default route for user VLAN traffic. Routers need routes to remote networks, which may be directly connected, statically configured, or learned through a routing protocol. Use show ip interface brief, show ip route, and ping to verify interface and path state.</p><div class="lesson-source"><span>SOURCE IMAGES</span><code>IMG-0692 · IMG-0738</code></div></div></section>
<section class="lesson-end"><div><Eyebrow label="CHECK YOUR UNDERSTANDING" /><h2>Basic Router Configuration</h2><p>Practice the key terms, procedures, and troubleshooting decisions from this module.</p></div><a href="/cisco/practice/">Start practice →</a></section>
<section class="lesson-section"><div class="lesson-number">04</div><div><h2>Interpret interface states and verify faults</h2><p>In a brief interface summary, up/up means the physical interface and line protocol are operational. Administratively down/down means the interface is shut down in configuration. Up/down means the physical layer is up but the line protocol is down; check the far end, encapsulation, keepalives, and counters. A status summary is a starting point, not a complete diagnosis. Use show interfaces and the relevant IPv4/IPv6 interface commands for detail.</p><div class="lesson-source"><span>VERIFICATION</span><code>show ip interface brief · show ipv6 interface brief · show interfaces</code></div></div></section>
<section class="lesson-section"><div class="lesson-number">05</div><div><h2>Apply and test a secure baseline</h2><p>A typical initial sequence is to set hostname and domain name, configure enable secret and a local username secret, secure the console line, generate RSA keys, configure VTY local authentication and SSH-only transport, set a legal banner, and save the running configuration with copy running-config startup-config. Saving does not reload the router or change the active running configuration. If changes are not saved, a reboot or power loss can discard them. Open a new management session to verify the banner and SSH access.</p><pre><code>hostname R1
ip domain-name example.local
enable secret &lt;strong-secret&gt;
username admin secret &lt;user-secret&gt;
crypto key generate rsa
line console 0
 login local
line vty 0 4
 login local
 transport input ssh
banner motd ^CAuthorized access only.^C
service password-encryption
copy running-config startup-config</code></pre><p>Command availability and VTY line ranges vary by IOS platform. Verify the actual device before applying a template.</p><div class="lesson-source"><span>SOURCE TOPICS</span><code>Initial router settings · Console/VTY · SSH · MOTD · Save configuration</code></div></div></section>
</section>
</SiteLayout>
